# WordPress PHP Standards

- Follow the project's WordPress Coding Standards and supported PHP/WordPress versions.
- Prefix or namespace global PHP symbols and all WordPress identifiers.
- Register behavior on the correct hook; do not perform request work at include time.
- Keep hook, REST, AJAX, shortcode, block-render and CLI callbacks thin.
- Use WordPress APIs before direct filesystem, HTTP, mail, cache, cron or database code.
- Sanitize and validate input; escape late for the exact output context.
- Check capability/object authorization separately from nonce/authentication.
- Every REST route has an intentional `permission_callback` and schema.
- Use `$wpdb->prepare()` for values and allowlist dynamic identifiers.
- Keep options small, avoid unbounded autoload/serialization, and make cache invalidation explicit.
- Version migrations and make activation, cron, webhook, import and background work idempotent.
- Preserve public hooks, routes, block attributes, option/meta formats, shortcodes and PHP APIs.
- State site versus network ownership and restore every multisite blog switch.
